**Answer:** 130.28 (heat loss per foot), 498.88 °F at the pipe‑insulation interface  

**Explanation**

The heat transfer through a cylindrical wall is governed by  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{2\pi L (T_i-T_o)}{\displaystyle \frac{\ln(r_o/r_i)}{k_{\text{pipe}}}
                                                     +\frac{\ln(r_{i,ins}/r_{o,pipe})}{k_{\text{ins}}}}
\]

where  

* \(r_i = \dfrac{6}{2}=3\) in = 0.25 ft (inner radius of pipe)  
* \(r_o = \dfrac{6.5}{2}=3.25\) in = 0.2708 ft (outer radius of pipe)  
* Insulation thickness = 3.5 in → \(r_{i,ins}=r_o\) and \(r_{o,ins}=r_o+3.5\text{ in}=6.75\text{ in}=0.5625 ft\)

Convert the thermal conductivities to consistent units (they are already in Btu hr⁻¹ ft⁻¹ °F⁻¹).

**Thermal resistances**

Pipe steel  

\[
R_{\text{pipe}}= \frac{\ln (r_o/r_i)}{2\pi k_{\text{pipe}}}
               =\frac{\ln(0.2708/0.25)}{2\pi(26)}
               =1.46\times10^{-3}\; \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}
\]

Insulation  

\[
R_{\text{ins}}= \frac{\ln (r_{o,ins}/r_{i,ins})}{2\pi k_{\text{ins}}}
              =\frac{\ln(0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi(0.04)}
              =1.86\times10^{-1}\; \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}
\]

Total resistance  

\[
R_{tot}=R_{\text{pipe}}+R_{\text{ins}} \approx 0.188\; \text{hr·ft·°F/Btu}
\]

**Heat loss per foot**

\[
\dot Q = \frac{T_{\text{inner}}-T_{\text{outer}}}{R_{tot}}
        =\frac{500-150}{0.188}
        \approx 130.28\; \frac{\text{energy}}{\text{hr·ft}}
\]

**Interface temperature**

The temperature drop across the steel pipe is  

\[
\Delta T_{\text{pipe}} = \dot Q \, R_{\text{pipe}}
                       =130.28 \times 1.46\times10^{-3}
                       \approx 0.19\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

Hence  

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{pipe}}
                     \approx 500 - 0.19 \approx 498.88^\circ\text{F}
\]

Thus the heat loss is about **130.28 per foot of pipe**, and the temperature at the pipe‑insulation boundary is **≈ 498.9 °F**, which matches choice **J**.<|return|>

