**Explanation**

For a cylindrical wall the heat‑transfer rate per unit length is  

\[
\dot Q = \frac{2\pi (T_i - T_o)}{ \displaystyle \frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{k_{\text{steel}}} + \frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{k_{\text{ins}}}}
\]

where  

* \(r_1 = \tfrac{6}{2}=3.0\) in. (inner radius of pipe)  
* \(r_2 = \tfrac{6.5}{2}=3.25\) in. (outer radius of pipe)  
* insulation thickness = 3.5 in., so \(r_3 = r_2 + 3.5 = 6.75\) in.  

Convert the radii to feet (1 ft = 12 in.):

\[
r_1 = 0.250\; \text{ft},\qquad
r_2 = 0.2708\; \text{ft},\qquad
r_3 = 0.5625\; \text{ft}
\]

Thermal conductivities:  
\(k_{\text{steel}} = 26\) (units as given)  
\(k_{\text{ins}}   = 0.04\)

Compute the resistances:

\[
R_{\text{steel}} = \frac{\ln(r_2/r_1)}{2\pi k_{\text{steel}}}
                 = \frac{\ln(0.2708/0.250)}{2\pi(26)} = 4.81\times10^{-5}\; \text{(ft·°F)/unit heat}
\]

\[
R_{\text{ins}}   = \frac{\ln(r_3/r_2)}{2\pi k_{\text{ins}}}
                 = \frac{\ln(0.5625/0.2708)}{2\pi(0.04)} = 7.88\times10^{-3}\; \text{(ft·°F)/unit heat}
\]

Total resistance per foot  

\[
R_{\text{tot}} = R_{\text{steel}} + R_{\text{ins}}
               \approx 7.93\times10^{-3}\; \text{(ft·°F)/unit heat}
\]

Temperature drop from the pipe interior (500 °F) to the outer insulation surface (150 °F) is  

\[
\Delta T = 500 - 150 = 350\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

