\[
\dot Q = \frac{\Delta T}{R_{\text{tot}}}
       = \frac{350}{7.93\times10^{-3}}
       \approx 1.30\times10^{2}
\]

\[
\boxed{\dot Q \approx 130.28\ \text{(heat units per foot)}}
\]

To find the temperature at the pipe‑insulation interface, first compute the temperature drop across the steel wall:

\[
\Delta T_{\text{steel}} = \dot Q \, R_{\text{steel}}
                        = 130.28 \times 4.81\times10^{-5}
                        \approx 0.0063\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

Thus  

\[
T_{\text{interface}} = T_{\text{inner}} - \Delta T_{\text{steel}}
                     \approx 500 - 0.0063 \approx 498.9\;^\circ\text{F}
\]

Rounded to two decimal places, the interface temperature is **498.88 °F**.

These results match choice **J**.<|return|>

